  "uT": 1,
  "summary": "Murphy's simple Shiva session 'amygdala and hippocampus signals in sequence' in its fast version, which the vendor says 'speeds the signal up by three times': an amygdala burst and a hippocampal burst take turns, stepped counter-clockwise around the head. Murphy says the pair 'should elicit a greater limbic activation than either of them alone'. Both waveforms are rebuilt from descriptions.",
  "setting": "Dark, quiet room, eyes closed, relaxed without meditating or falling asleep. Shiva offers 20, 30 and 60-min versions; start with the short one.",
  "schedule": "At most once a week, never on consecutive days; up to 6 sessions, then a 21-day break.",
  "notes": "The vendor: the two signals 'are presented one after the other', and 'fast speeds the signal up by three times' (amygdala_hippocampus_page.htm). Neither vendor file is published: both signals are rebuilt and played at 1-ms points instead of 3 ms. Amygdala: this project's rising sweep with the 289 points of the Shiva amygdaloid signal (Saroka 2010 p.5 [470]; Collins 2014 p.40 [31]): 289 ms, 18 to 90 Hz. Hippocampus: a primed burst, one pulse, then four pulses 57 ms later, three times in 0.25 s (at normal speed 170 ms and 0.74 s, after Tsang 2004 p.5 [1017]). Each signal is followed by 1.33 s of silence, this project's choice: whether the fast version shortens the 4-s silence is not stated. One cycle takes 3.2 s. The eight coils sit on the vendor's Shiva ring and fire one at a time, counter-clockwise seen from above. Shiva has a coil pair at each position; one coil keeps the timing and covers about half the arc. The step of 10, 12 ... 20 ms per coil is this project's reading of a lecture example with no unit named (Murphy lecture 19:42-19:53). This session's step table is not published; lab runs of the amygdala signal used other tables (Saroka 2010 p.5 [470]; Collins 2014 p.40 [31]). The six values restart every six coils, but the vendor describes eight segments (shiva_explanation.htm). The vendor gives no number for Shiva's field. This program's setting is the peak field 20 mm in front of each coil. At 1 uT that is about 24 uT at the scalp. The effect is a vendor claim. Independent sham-controlled tests of God-Helmet-type fields found no field effect (Granqvist 2005, double-blind; Gendle & McGrath 2012, where the experimenter knew the condition). Try murphy-shiva-amy-hipp-sequence first. Not for people with epilepsy; stop if distressed. Murphy reports headaches in about 2% of users of an earlier version, mostly with hippocampal signals: lower the intensity by a third.",
  "targets": ["left-amygdala", "right-amygdala", "left-hippocampus-head", "left-hippocampus-body", "left-hippocampus-tail", "right-hippocampus-head", "right-hippocampus-body", "right-hippocampus-tail"],
  "source": "Murphy, Shiva demo: Amygdala and hippocampus signals in sequence https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/shiva/shiva_demo/amygdala_hippocampus_page.htm ; Murphy, Shakti demo: What are 'Rapid' and 'High-Speed' signals? https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/winshakti/demo/shakti_demo/rapid_&_high_speed_signals.htm ; Murphy, Mood enhancement / feelgood (8 Coil Shakti; two signals 'one after the other' with a four-second latency) https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/winshakti/rotating/shakti_happiness.htm ; Murphy, Shakti demo: Random Theta Band output (LTP spectral peak 5.4 Hz) https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/winshakti/demo/shakti_demo/5jut4ranconsent.htm ; Murphy, Shiva demo: Consumer information https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/shiva/shiva_demo/shiva_infomed_consent.htm ; Murphy, Shiva demo: Magnetic coil locations https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/shiva/shiva_demo/locations.htm ; Murphy, Psychic Skills & Miracles lecture (coil steps of 10, 12 ... 20 ms) https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tmC1174POpA ; Murphy, Shiva demo: Session lengths https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/shiva/shiva_demo/session_length.htm ; Ryczko & Persinger 2002, Percept Mot Skills 95:592 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12434855/ ; Granqvist et al. 2005, Neurosci Lett 379:1 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15849873/ ; Gendle & McGrath 2012, Percept Mot Skills 114:217 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22582690/",
